00:00:00.019 --> 00:00:02.310
MAX SALTONSTALL: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.650
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.410
So if you didn't see Shield Your
Cloud with Verifiable Advanced

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.147
Platform Security,
then stay tuned

00:00:09.147 --> 00:00:10.230
because here's the rewind.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.158
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.136
If you're like me,
you probably run

00:00:19.136 --> 00:00:20.760
a bunch of virtual
machines, and you're

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.968
using a mix of your own
hardware, some public clouded

00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:25.110
resources, maybe
both, and of course

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.790
you want to keep them safe.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.580
But are they safe?

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.620
Are you sure?

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.930
One major challenge with
this is just how quickly

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.610
we see attackers
jump into action

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.740
with publicly accessible VMs.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.020
We notice attempts to
penetrate new virtual machines

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.350
in under five minutes once
they're publicly exposed.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.570
An attacker wants
to get to your data,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.700
take over your resources,
or even better,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.158
get your credentials,
and then they

00:00:49.158 --> 00:00:50.460
can easily do both at once.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.470
To help keep your VM
safe, Google Cloud

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.090
provides shielded VMs.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.220
This new option can
protect your resources

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.670
and give you assurances that
your virtual machines only run

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.640
in a safe, secure,
and expected state.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.470
How does that work?

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.080
A shielded VM extends the
defense in depth model

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.260
to the very lowest level,
establishing a root of trust

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.400
with the boot loader
and the firmware.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.510
This is important because if
you can't trust your OS, then

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.580
you can't trust the things
running on top of it--

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.950
your intrusion
detection, audit logging,

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.750
privileges separation,
or antivirus-- at all.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.760
So the trust needs to start
before the OS even begins,

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.830
which is why we need a
hardware root of trust

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:32.500
to keep these fundamental
components safe.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.870
Enter Titan, a chip
designed by Google

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.430
to secure the boot process.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.350
Each Titan chip as it gets
made has a machine identity key

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.320
burned into it and
can further attest

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.410
that it has the correct firmware
running on itself and its host

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.730
machine.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.480
Titan also validates
the signature

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.170
of the boot loader
and kernel binaries,

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.630
so it will stop boot if the
signatures don't check out.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.940
This lets shielded
VMs assure integrity

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.670
from the very first
instruction executed.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.490
We go beyond secure boot
and give you measured boot,

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.980
providing insight and
control into the entire boot

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.760
sequence from firmware to boot
loader to kernel binaries.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.289
Putting all this
together allows you

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:11.020
to validate a virtual
machine as a safe environment

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.900
for your data, your
code, and your secrets.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.300
We think everyone
should use shielded VMs.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.310
There's virtually no
performance impact.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.170
They reduce the
trusted compute base.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.150
They fully support live
migration and patching,

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.160
and they work
across any hardware.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.160
